## Rendering Data into the DOM with .map()

#### When we render data on a web page, we often start with an array of objects. EX: users, products, or messages.
#### `.map()` allows us to loop through that data and turn each object into a piece of HTML, which we can then display on the page.

#### **In other words:**

- #### Each item in the data becomes one visual element

- #### `.map()` helps us create those elements consistently and efficiently

- #### This pattern shows up constantly in real-world web development.

<div
  style="
    display: flex;
    gap: 16px;
    margin: 20px 0;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  "
>
  <!-- Card 1 -->
  <div
    style="
      flex: 1;
      background: #f7f8fa;
      border-radius: 12px;
      padding: 16px;
      box-shadow: 2px 4px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2);
      min-width: 280px;
    "
  >
    <h2 style="margin-top: 0;">✅ What <code>.map()</code> Does</h2>

<p style="font-size: 14pt;">
      <code>.map()</code> is a built-in JavaScript array method that:
    </p>

<ul style="font-size: 14pt;">
      <li>Loops through every item in an array</li>
      <li>Applies a function to each item</li>
      <li>Returns a new array with the results</li>
      <li>Does <strong>not</strong> change the original array</li>
    </ul>


  </div>

  <!-- Card 2 -->
  <div
    style="
      flex: 1;
      background: #f7f8fa;
      border-radius: 12px;
      padding: 16px;
      box-shadow: 2px 4px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2);
      min-width: 280px;
    "
  >
    <h2 style="margin-top: 0;">🧠 Why <code>.map()</code> Is Useful</h2>

<p style="font-size: 14pt;">
      <code>.map()</code> makes it easy to transform data, which is essential when working with the DOM.
    </p>

<p style="font-size: 14pt;">It’s especially useful for:</p>

<ul style="font-size: 14pt;">
      <li>Doubling or modifying numbers</li>
      <li>Converting or formatting data</li>
      <li>Pulling specific properties from objects</li>
      <li>Turning data into HTML elements for display</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>


  <div style="text-align: center; width: 100%;">

  <div style="display: inline-block; background-color: #efe7f8; border: 2px solid #8c52ba; border-radius: 12px; padding: 20px 30px; max-width: 700px; box-shadow: 0 2px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <p style="font-size: 1.2rem; margin: 0; line-height: 1.5; color: #3b0066;">
       Think of <code>.map()</code> as:<br>
       <em>“Take each element, do something to it, and give me a new list.”</em>
    </p>
  </div>

</div>


